// NIST P-256 (secp256r1, FIPS 186-4 D.1.2.3) as an instance of the curve-generic ECDSA verifier in
// src/neant/crypto/ec.nt. Not part of the boot image — load all four:
//   load "src/neant/crypto/bignum.nt"   load "src/neant/crypto/der.nt"
//   load "src/neant/crypto/ec.nt"       load "src/neant/crypto/p256.nt"
//
//   ecdsaVerifyP256[pub;digest;r;s]    -> 1b / 0b   pub is the SEC 1 point x509.nt's `spki`point
//                                                   hands you; r and s are big-endian magnitudes
//   ecdsaVerifyP256Der[pub;digest;sig] -> 1b / 0b   sig is DER SEQUENCE { INTEGER r, INTEGER s }
//
// `digest` is the already-computed hash of the signed message and must be exactly 32 bytes: this
// verifier is ECDSA-with-SHA-256 and nothing else. ec.nt's header is where every check, every
// refusal and the reasoning behind them is written down; this file is the domain parameters and
// the names. The arithmetic below is ec.nt's, wrapped so that P256C never has to be spelled at a
// call site and so that fpMul, p256Dbl and the rest go on meaning what they meant when this file
// WAS the arithmetic — tests/p256.nt and verify.nt call them by those names.
//
// ---- the field, and why this reuses bignum.nt instead of folding the special prime
// p = 2^256 - 2^224 + 2^192 + 2^96 - 1 is a Solinas prime, and the textbook reduction for it (FIPS
// 186-4 D.2) is nine 32-bit-word add/subtracts and no division — the same shape of trick that
// crypto.nt plays on 2^255-19 and 2^130-5. It does not pay off here, and the reason is the i64:
//   * every exponent in p is a multiple of 32, so the nine terms are limb permutations only when
//     the limb width divides 32. A column of n products of w-bit limbs must stay under 2^62 to be
//     exact in an int, which caps w at 16 — so the Solinas basis is 16 limbs of 16 bits, against
//     the 10 limbs of 26 bits bignum.nt already uses. 16 multiply-accumulate passes, not 10.
//   * the fold cannot be crypto.nt's `9728 * the high columns`: 2^256 = 2^224 - 2^192 - 2^96 + 1
//     mod p is sparse but not small, so it is a permutation-and-add, not a scalar multiply.
// Measured on this machine (./target/release/neant, one 256-bit modular multiplication):
//   bnMontMul, 10 limbs of 26 bits                       25 us
//   the same loop written out fixed-width, no pad/trim   21 us
//   16-bit Solinas: schoolbook multiply alone            11 us
//   16-bit Solinas: multiply + the nine-term fold        19 us   (and it still has to bring the
//     result, which lands in roughly (-4p, 6p), back under 2^256 — four compare-and-subtract
//     rounds on 16-limb vectors, about 5 us more)
// So the dedicated reduction comes out level with the general one, because the extra six
// multiply-accumulate passes that 16-bit limbs cost are worth more than the division the fold
// saves — and bnMontMul saves no division either, REDC is the whole point of it. Level means
// reuse: neither this file nor ec.nt adds arithmetic of its own, and bignum.nt's Montgomery form is
// what every field element is in. It is also what made a SECOND curve nearly free: see p384.nt.
//
// ---- what it costs, measured on this machine (src/main.rs, mod p256, mod p384 and mod rsa, by
// `cargo test --release -- --nocapture`)
//   ECDSA P-256 verification    180 ms
//   ECDSA P-384 verification    380 ms   the same code, a longer curve record: see p384.nt
//   RSA-2048 PKCS#1 v1.5        7.8 ms
// A verification is one inversion mod n, 256 Jacobian doublings and about 192 mixed additions over
// Shamir's trick, and one inversion mod p to come back to affine: roughly 4900 modular
// multiplications, against the twenty an RSA-2048 exponentiation with e = 65537 costs. Twenty-odd
// times the work for twenty-odd times the time is the whole explanation — and it is the reverse of
// the ratio a compiled implementation shows, where P-256 beats RSA-2048 handily, because there each
// modular multiplication is a handful of instructions and here it is a dozen interpreted vector
// operations on ten-element lists. Nothing about the algorithm is at fault; the constant is.
// Two shapes that look like they should help and do not, both counted before being written off:
//   * a window-2 Shamir (128 rounds of two doublings and one addition) saves about 72 mixed
//     additions, 790 multiplications — and spends about 630 building a fifteen-entry table and
//     batch-inverting it to affine, because one inversion IS 384 multiplications here. Three per
//     cent, for a table, a batch inversion and fifteen more degenerate cases to get right.
//   * checking X = r*Z^2 mod p instead of inverting Z and reducing x mod n would save one of the
//     two inversions, but the comparison then has to handle x >= n by hand, and that is the one
//     equation in ECDSA a verifier must not get subtly wrong. 5% is not worth it.
//
// ---- inversion: Fermat, not extended Euclid
// efInv and the s^-1 mod n in ec.nt both go through bnModExpL to the p-2 / n-2 power: 255 squarings
// and one multiply per set bit — p-2 has 128 of them and n-2 about 160, so 384 and 415 modular
// multiplications. A binary extended Euclid would take roughly 512 iterations of shifts and
// conditional subtracts — fewer elementary operations, but each iteration is an interpreted loop
// body with branches, and bignum.nt has no signed representation to run it in, so it would mean new
// arithmetic in a file this task may not restructure. The two inversions are ~800 of the ~4900
// modular multiplications a verification costs, 16% of it: halving them would buy 8%, which is not
// worth a second modular-arithmetic path in a verifier that has to be read before it is trusted.

// ---- the domain parameters (FIPS 186-4 D.1.2.3; `openssl ecparam -name prime256v1
// -param_enc explicit -text` prints exactly these). a = p-3, which is what ec.nt's doubling
// assumes; the cofactor is 1; the digest is 32 octets, because this is ECDSA-with-SHA-256.
P256C: ecCurve ("ffffffff00000001000000000000000000000000ffffffffffffffffffffffff"
  ; "ffffffff00000000ffffffffffffffffbce6faada7179e84f3b9cac2fc632551"
  ; "5ac635d8aa3a93e7b3ebbd55769886bc651d06b0cc53b0f63bce3c3e27d2604b"
  ; "6b17d1f2e12c4247f8bce6e563a440f277037d812deb33a0f4a13945d898c296"
  ; "4fe342e2fe1a7f9b8ee7eb4a7c0f9e162bce33576b315ececbb6406837bf51f5"; 32)

// the same names this file exported when the parameters were spelled out one global at a time
P256P:   P256C`p
P256N:   P256C`n
P256NP:  P256C`np                                         // -p^-1 mod 2^26, for bnMontMul
P256R2:  P256C`r2                                         // R^2 mod p, R = 2^260
P256PM2: P256C`pm2
P256NM2: P256C`nm2
FP0: BNZERO
FP1: P256C`one                                            // R mod p, the Montgomery form of 1
FPB: P256C`b
P256G: (P256C`gx; P256C`gy)
P256INF: ecInf P256C

// ---- the field mod p, and the group in Jacobian coordinates: ec.nt's, with the curve bound.
// Every value is in Montgomery form; fpTo and fpFrom are the only two places that shows.
fpMul: {[a;b] efMul[P256C;a;b]}
fpSqr: {[a] efSqr[P256C;a]}
fpTo:  {[x] efTo[P256C;x]}                                // x -> xR
fpFrom:{[x] efFrom[P256C;x]}                              // xR -> x
fpAdd: {[a;b] efAdd[P256C;a;b]}
fpSub: {[a;b] efSub[P256C;a;b]}
fpInv: {[a] efInv[P256C;a]}
p256IsInf:    {[P] ecIsInf P}
p256Dbl:      {[P] ecDbl[P256C;P]}
p256MAdd:     {[P;A] ecMAdd[P256C;P;A]}
p256ToAffine: {[P] ecToAffine[P256C;P]}
p256Bits:     {[v] ecBits[P256C;v]}
p256Shamir:   {[b1;A1;b2;A2] ecShamir[P256C;b1;A1;b2;A2]}
p256Point:    {[b] ecPoint[P256C;b]}

// ---- the two exports, total: every malformed input comes back 0b. ec.nt's header says why.
ecdsaVerifyP256:    {[pub;digest;r;s] ecdsaVerify[P256C;pub;digest;r;s]}
ecdsaVerifyP256Der: {[pub;digest;sig] ecdsaVerifyDer[P256C;pub;digest;sig]}
